    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a pilot formulation for a low-fat mayonnaise variant, but I'm running into some stability issues with the emulsion in high-acid environments (pH ~3.8). Could you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface under these acidic conditions, and how the charge on the emulsifier might be affecting the droplet coalescence?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In low-fat mayonnaise, lecithin ( العسيرا ) plays a crucial role in stabilizing the oil-water interface due to its amphiphilic nature. Its phosphorylcholine headgroup has a slight negative charge at pH 3.8, allowing it to interact with the negatively charged egg lecithin that naturally occurs in the egg yolks, while its nonpolar tails provide hydrophobic interaction with the oil. This electrostatic attraction helps to prevent coalescence and stabilize the droplet interface, reducing the likelihood of separation. Additionally, lecithin's hydrophobic tails also reduce the overall surface tension of the interface, further contributing to its emulsive properties.",
      "source": "target",
